## Proof

**Claim.** For every $h \ge 0$ there exists a measure-preserving transformation whose Kolmogorov–Sinai entropy equals $h$.

We work throughout with entropy measured in bits (logarithms base $2$); the argument is unit-independent since changing the log base only rescales.

### Step 1. A continuous family of transformations realizing every entropy in $[0,1]$.

For $p \in [0,\tfrac12]$, let $B(p)$ denote the **two-symbol Bernoulli shift**: on the probability space $(\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{Z}}, \mu_p)$, where $\mu_p$ is the product measure giving each coordinate the value $1$ with probability $p$ and $0$ with probability $1-p$, the transformation is the left shift $\sigma$. It is measure-preserving, and by the Kolmogorov–Sinai theorem its entropy is the entropy of the generating partition:
$$
\mathbf{h}(p) \;=\; -p\log_2 p - (1-p)\log_2(1-p),
$$
with the convention $0\log 0 = 0$.

The function $\mathbf{h}:[0,\tfrac12]\to\mathbb{R}$ is continuous, $\mathbf{h}(0)=0$, and $\mathbf{h}(\tfrac12)=1$. By the **Intermediate Value Theorem**, for every $r\in[0,1]$ there exists $p_r\in[0,\tfrac12]$ with
$$
\mathbf{h}(p_r)=r.
$$
Thus $B(p_r)$ is a measure-preserving transformation of entropy exactly $r$. In particular $B(0)$ is the identity (entropy $0$) and $B(\tfrac12)$ is the fair-coin shift (entropy $1$).

### Step 2. Entropy is additive under independent products.

Let $T_1,\dots,T_k$ be measure-preserving transformations on probability spaces $(X_i,\mu_i)$. The product $T = T_1\times\cdots\times T_k$ acts on $(X_1\times\cdots\times X_k,\,\mu_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\mu_k)$ by $T(x_1,\dots,x_k)=(T_1 x_1,\dots,T_k x_k)$. It is measure-preserving, and a standard consequence of the Kolmogorov–Sinai theorem (the generating partition of the product is the join of the generating partitions of the factors) gives
$$
h_{\mathrm{KS}}(T_1\times\cdots\times T_k) \;=\; h_{\mathrm{KS}}(T_1)+\cdots+h_{\mathrm{KS}}(T_k).
$$

### Step 3. Realizing an arbitrary $h\ge 0$.

Given $h\ge 0$, write
$$
h = n + r,\qquad n=\lfloor h\rfloor\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0},\quad r\in[0,1).
$$
Choose $p_r\in[0,\tfrac12]$ with $\mathbf{h}(p_r)=r$ (Step 1) and form the finite product
$$
T \;=\; \underbrace{B(\tfrac12)\times\cdots\times B(\tfrac12)}_{n\text{ factors}}\;\times\; B(p_r).
$$
(When $r=0$, $B(p_r)=B(0)$ is the identity and may be dropped; when $n=0$ the first block is absent.) By Step 2,
$$
h_{\mathrm{KS}}(T) \;=\; n\cdot \mathbf{h}(\tfrac12) + \mathbf{h}(p_r) \;=\; n\cdot 1 + r \;=\; n+r \;=\; h.
$$

Since $h\ge 0$ was arbitrary, **for every nonnegative number $h$ there exists a measure-preserving transformation whose entropy equals $h$**. $\blacksquare$

